ditions interact globally. The convolution is strictly **non-causal** (bidirectional) and circular/zero-padded:\n   $$y_1 = h_1 * v_0 = \\mathcal{F}^{-1}\\left( \\mathcal{F}(\\tilde{h}_1) \\odot \\mathcal{F}(\\tilde{v}_0) \\right)$$\n   where $\\mathcal{F}$ is the 1D Discrete Fourier Transform along sequence dimension $T$, computed via Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) in $\\mathcal{O}(T \\log T)$.\n4. **Hierarchical Gating:**\n   $$v_1 = x_1 \\odot y_1, \\quad y_2 = h_2 * v_1, \\quad y_{\\text{out}} = W_{\\text{out}} (x_2 \\odot y_2)$$\n\n#### Rationale for the Non-Causal Hyena Operator\n* **Sub-Quadratic Scaling:** Reduces temporal mixing cost from $\\mathcal{O}(T^2)$ to $\\mathcal{O}(T \\log T)$, unlocking sequence lengths $T \\ge 16384$ without chunking.\n* **Global Receptive Field at Layer 1:** Unlike windowed attention or local convolutions, the implicit filter spans the entire domain $[-T/2, T/2]$ in every layer, capturing long-term planetary or macroscopic modes.\n* **Multi-Scale Frequency Representation:** The implicit filter MLP operates as a continuous spectral synthesizer. By learning both high-frequency Fourier modes and slow decay envelopes, it naturally captures fast oscillations and slow drift simultaneously.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Experimental Controls, Ablations, and Normalization Protocols\n\nTo isolate the causal effect of substituting the temporal mechanism, all models are trained under strictly controlled, identical conditions:\n\n### 2.1 Model Configurations and Parameter Matching\nBecause Hyena replaces three projection matrices $(Q, K, V)$ with input projections and a small filter MLP, parameter counts naturally differ if widths are held constant. We calibrate hidden dimensions to ensure equal capacity:\n\n| Model Variant | Setup ID / Role | Temporal Operator | Hidden Dim $D$ | Layers $L$ | Parameter Count |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Baseline SiT** | `setup_4` (Baseline) | Full Bidirectional Attention (8 heads) | 512 | 12 | $\\sim 52.4\\text{ M}$ |\n| **Hyena-SiT** | `setup_5` (Intervention) | Non-Causal Hyena (Order 2, Filter MLP width 64) | 544 | 12 | $\\sim 52.6\\text{ M}$ |\n| **Causal Hyena-SiT** | Ablation Control 1 | Causal Hyena (Masked FFT convolution) | 544 | 12 | $\\sim 52.6\\text{ M}$ |\n| **Windowed SiT** | Ablation Control 2 | Local Temporal Attention (Window $W=256$) | 512 | 12 | $\\sim 52.4\\text{ M}$ |\n| **Bi-Mamba-SiT** | Ablation Control 3 | Bidirectional State Space Model (Mamba) | 512 | 12 | $\\sim 52.5\\text{ M}$ |\n\n### 2.2 Ablation Rationales\n1. **Causal Hyena-SiT:** Disentangles the contribution of non-causal (bidirectional) context from the convolutional parameterization. Validates whether bidirectional information flow is necessary for interpolant trajectory consistency.\n2. **Windowed SiT:** Matches the sub-quadratic computational budget of Hyena using standard attention. Disentangles the benefit of low FLOP count from the presence of a global receptive field.\n3. **Bi-Mamba-SiT:** Compares the implicit long convolution against an input-dependent recurrent state-space mechanism.\n\n### 2.3 Training Normalization Protocol\n* **Objective:** Standard conditional flow matching velocity loss with identical linear interpolant schedule.\n* **Optimization:** AdamW ($\\beta_1=0.9, \\beta_2=0.999$, weight decay $10^{-2}$).\n* **Learning Rate:** Base LR $2 \\times 10^{-4}$ with 5,000 warmup steps followed by cosine annealing.\n* **Batch Sizing & Gradient Accumulation:** Fixed effective batch size of 64 trajectories across all models.\n* **Inference Integrator:** Standard 4th-order Runge-Kutta (RK4) or Heun ODE solver with a fixed 100 evaluation steps over $t \\in [0, 1]$.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Testbed Dynamical Systems and Data Generation\n\nWe evaluate across three dynamical regimes exhibiting multi-timescale behavior, spatial-temporal chaos, and strict conservation laws:\n\n```\n\u250c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n\u2502                        DYNAMICAL BENCHMARKS                            \u2502\n\u251c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u252c\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2524\n\u2502 1. Two-Scale L-96   \u2502 2. Kuramoto-Sivashinsky  \u2502 3. 2D Kolmogorov Flow \u2502\n\u2502 (Coupled Multi-ODE) \u2502 (1D Spatiotemporal PDE)  \u2502 (2D Incompressible NS)\u2502\n\u2502 Slow/Fast Climate   \u2502 Micro-instability &      \u2502 Inverse energy cascade\u2502\n\u2502 waves & convection  \u2502 Macro flame front        \u2502 & vortex mergers      \u2502\n\u2514\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2534\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n```\n\n### 3.1 Benchmark 1: Two-Scale Lorenz-96 System (Atmospheric Multi-Scale Dynamics)\n* **Mathematical Formulation:**\n  $$\\frac{dX_k}{dt} = -X_{k-1}(X_{k-2} - X_{k+1}) - X_k + F - \\frac{h c}{b} \\sum_{j=1}^J Y_{j, k}, \\quad k \\in \\{1, \\dots, K\\}$$\n  $$\\frac{dY_{j,k}}{dt} = -c b Y_{j+1, k}(Y_{j+2, k} - Y_{j-1, k}) - c Y_{j, k} + \\frac{h c}{b} X_k, \\quad j \\in \\{1, \\dots, J\\}$$\n* **Parameters:** $K=8$ slow variables (synoptic scale), $J=32$ fast variables per slow variable ($256$ fast variables total, convective scale), forcing $F=18.0$, coupling $h=1.0$, timescale separation $c=10.0$, spatial scale ratio $b=10.0$.\n* **Properties:** Explicitly separates fast convective oscillations ($\\tau_{\\text{fast}} \\approx 0.05$) from slow large-scale wave regimes ($\\tau_{\\text{slow}} \\approx 0.5 - 2.0$).\n\n### 3.2 Benchmark 2: 1D Kuramoto-Sivashinsky (KS) Equation (Nonlinear Spatiotemporal Chaos)\n* **Mathematical Formulation:**\n  $$\\partial_t u + u \\partial_x u + \\partial_x^2 u + \\nu \\partial_x^4 u = 0, \\quad x \\in [0, 2\\pi L], \\text{ periodic BC}$$\n* **Parameters:** Domain size $L=22 / \\pi$, damping viscosity $\\nu=1.0$, discretized on $S=128$ spatial points using pseudo-spectral integration.\n* **Properties:** Negative diffusion $\\partial_x^2 u$ drives high-frequency instability; hyperviscosity $\\partial_x^4 u$ provides small-scale dissipation; nonlinear advection $u \\partial_x u$ transfers energy across scales, creating chaotic cellular dynamics with both short-term oscillations and long-term envelope patterns.\n\n### 3.3 Benchmark 3: 2D Kolmogorov / Navier-Stokes Flow (Turbulent Energy Cascade)\n* **Mathematical Formulation:**\n  $$\\partial_t \\omega + (u \\cdot \\nabla)\\omega = \\frac{1}{\\text{Re}}\\nabla^2 \\omega + n \\cos(n y), \\quad \\nabla \\cdot u = 0$$\n  where $\\omega = \\nabla \\times u$ is vorticity, $n=4$ (forcing wavenumber), Reynolds number $\\text{Re} = 1000$.\n* **Grid & Resolution:** $64 \\times 64$ spatial grid, pseudo-spectral solver with dealiasing.\n* **Properties:** Non-linear triad interactions yield an inverse energy cascade, transferring energy from forced intermediate scales to large coherent vortices over slow timescales, with fast turbulent vortex shedding at small scales.\n\n### 3.4 Horizon Scaling Protocols\nTo test trajectory scaling limits, each system is prepared across four temporal sequence lengths:\n* $T_1 = 512$ steps (Baseline manageable regime)\n* $T_2 = 2048$ steps (Transition regime, memory limits for dense attention begin)\n* $T_3 = 8192$ steps (Extremely long horizon)\n* $T_4 = 16384$ steps (Ultra-long horizon, testing multi-timescale coherence and stability)\n\n---\n\n## 4. Comprehensive Analysis Plan\n\n### 4.1 Multi-Timescale Dynamics Metrics\n\n1. **Power Spectral Density (PSD) Error across Frequency Bands:**\n   Compute the temporal Fourier transform of generated trajectories: $S(\\omega) = \\frac{1}{T}\\left| \\sum_{t=1}^T X(t) e^{-i \\omega t} \\right|^2$.\n   Partition the frequency spectrum into three distinct octaves:\n   * **Low-Frequency Band ($\\omega < \\omega_{\\text{low}}$):** Measures secular trends, slow circulation, and regime transitions.\n   * **Mid-Frequency Band ($\\omega_{\\text{low}} \\le \\omega \\le \\omega_{\\text{high}}$):** Measures dominant periodic cycles and wave propagation.\n   * **High-Frequency Band ($\\omega > \\omega_{\\text{high}}$):** Measures turbulent dissipation and fast convective fluctuations.\n   * Metric: Normalized Spectral Distance $\\Delta_{\\text{PSD}} = \\int_{\\Omega} \\left| \\log_{10} S_{\\text{gen}}(\\omega) - \\log_{10} S_{\\text{true}}(\\omega) \\right| d\\omega$.\n\n2. **Continuous Wavelet Transform (CWT) Scalogram Correlation:**\n   Apply Morlet wavelets to evaluate localized time-frequency energy bursts. Measures whether high-frequency energy is properly phase-modulated by slow-frequency cycles (cross-frequency coupling).\n\n3. **Temporal Autocorrelation Function (ACF) Alignment:**\n   $$R(\\tau) = \\frac{\\mathbb{E}_t \\left[ (X_t - \\mu)(X_{t+\\tau} - \\mu) \\right]}{\\sigma^2}$$\n   Evaluate $R(\\tau)$ up to lag $\\tau = T/2$. Measure the decorrelation time $\\tau_0$ (first zero-crossing) and the mean absolute error $\\text{MAE}_{\\text{ACF}} = \\frac{1}{\\tau_{\\max}} \\sum_{\\tau=1}^{\\tau_{\\max}} |R_{\\text{gen}}(\\tau) - R_{\\text{true}}(\\tau)|$.\n\n---\n\n### 4.2 Long-Term Trajectory Consistency and Attractor Invariants\n\n1. **Phase-Space Attractor Reconstruction and Lyapunov Spectra:**\n   * Reconstruct phase portraits using delay embedding coordinates: $\\mathbf{s}(t) = [X(t), X(t+\\tau_d), \\dots, X(t+(d-1)\\tau_d)]$.\n   * Estimate the Maximum Lyapunov Exponent (MLE) $\\lambda_1$:\n     $$\\lambda_1 = \\lim_{t \\to \\infty} \\frac{1}{t} \\ln \\frac{\\|\\delta \\mathbf{s}(t)\\|}{\\|\\delta \\mathbf{s}(0)\\|}$$\n     Alignment between $\\lambda_{1, \\text{gen}}$ and $\\lambda_{1, \\text{true}}$ verifies whether the generated model preserves chaotic sensitivity without collapsing to fixed points or drifting into unphysical blow-up.\n\n2. **Invariant Measure Alignment (Attractor Ergodicity):**\n   * Compute the Wasserstein-1 distance $\\mathcal{W}_1$ and Maximum Mean Discrepancy (MMD) with a Gaussian RBF kernel between long-term generated trajectory state distributions and true attractor distributions:\n     $$\\text{MMD}^2(p_{\\text{data}}, p_{\\theta}) = \\mathbb{E}[k(x, x')] - 2\\mathbb{E}[k(x, y)] + \\mathbb{E}[k(y, y')]$$\n\n3. **Trajectory Drift and Temporal Dispersion:**\n   Compute the variance of the state as a function of trajectory step $t \\in [1, T]$: $\\sigma^2(t) = \\text{Var}_S(X_t)$. Stable models maintain a stationary variance profile $\\sigma^2(t) \\approx \\text{const}$, whereas decaying models suffer variance collapse and unstable models exhibit variance explosion.\n\n---\n\n### 4.3 Physical Invariants and Conservation Laws\n\n1. **Energy and Enstrophy Drift:**\n   * Total kinetic energy: $E(t) = \\frac{1}{2} \\int |u(x, t)|^2 dx$.\n   * Enstrophy (2D Navier-Stokes): $\\Omega(t) = \\frac{1}{2} \\int |\\omega(x, t)|^2 dx$.\n   * Metric: Normalized conservation error over horizon $T$:\n     $$\\Delta_{\\text{cons}} = \\frac{1}{T} \\sum_{t=1}^T \\frac{|E(t) - E(0)|}{E(0)}$$\n2. **Solenoidal Incompressibility Condition:**\n   Check divergence-free velocity field: $\\|\\nabla \\cdot u_t\\|_2^2$ across all $t$.\n\n---\n\n### 4.4 Computational Efficiency and Scaling Benchmarks\n\n1. **Peak VRAM Allocation (GB):** Measured via `torch.cuda.max_memory_allocated()` during both training (forward + backward pass) and inference (100 ODE flow-matching generation steps) across sequence lengths $T \\in \\{512, 1024, 2048, 4096, 8192, 16384\\}$.\n2. **Throughput and Wall-Clock Latency:**\n   * Training throughput: Trajectory tokens processed per second ($\\text{samples} \\times T \\times S / \\text{sec}$).\n   * Generation wall-clock latency per complete trajectory.\n3. **Empirical Complexity Curves:** Fit scaling models $c_1 T^\\gamma$ to empirical runtimes to verify $\\gamma \\approx 1.0$ (for Hyena with FFT) versus $\\gamma \\approx 2.0$ (for full temporal attention).\n\n---\n\n## 5. Feasibility Assessment and Implementation Protocols\n\n### 5.1 Practical Implementation Architecture\n* **Framework:** PyTorch 2.x with CUDA 12.x.\n* **Fast Fourier Transform Engine:** `torch.fft.rfft` and `torch.fft.irfft` for real-valued trajectory sequences, ensuring memory-efficient conjugate-symmetric transforms.\n* **Filter Parameterization:** Filter MLP consists of 3 hidden layers of width 64 with sinusoidal activations (SIREN-like or random Fourier features) to resolve sharp high-frequency modes while preserving smooth macroscopic envelopes.\n* **Boundary Conditions & Padding:** Bidirectional convolution requires doubling the sequence length via symmetric or circular zero-padding ($2T$) prior to FFT to avoid circular wrap-around artifacts, followed by unpadding to $T$.\n\n### 5.2 Compute and Hardware Budget\n* **Hardware Setup:** 4 $\\times$ NVIDIA A100 (80GB SXM4) GPUs interconnected via NVLink.\n* **Training Feasibility Matrix:**\n\n| Horizon Length $T$ | Baseline SiT (`setup_4`) | Hyena-SiT (`setup_5`) | Feasibility Outcome |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **$T = 512$** | Feasible ($\\sim 14$ GB VRAM) | Feasible ($\\sim 11$ GB VRAM) | Both train within standard memory budgets. |\n| **$T = 2048$** | Marginal ($\\sim 58$ GB VRAM) | Feasible ($\\sim 16$ GB VRAM) | Baseline requires activation checkpointing. |\n| **$T = 8192$** | **OOM** (Out of Memory > 80GB) | Feasible ($\\sim 28$ GB VRAM) | Attention fails completely; Hyena executes smoothly. |\n| **$T = 16384$** | **OOM** | Feasible ($\\sim 46$ GB VRAM) | Only Hyena supports end-to-end trajectory generation. |\n\n### 5.3 Potential Pitfalls and Mitigation Strategies\n1. **Numerical Stability in Long FFT Convolutions:**\n   * *Risk:* Floating-point precision loss in single-precision FP32/BF16 over sequence lengths of $T \\ge 16384$.\n   * *Mitigation:* Execute FFT and IFFT operations in `torch.float32` even if mixed-precision AMP (`bfloat16`) is used for the linear projections.\n2. **High-Frequency Spectral Aliasing:**\n   * *Risk:* Filter MLP generating frequencies exceeding the Nyquist limit of the temporal grid.\n   * *Mitigation:* Apply an anti-aliasing low-pass Gaussian taper at the Nyquist frequency during filter synthesis.\n3. **Boundary Reflection Artifacts:**\n   * *Risk:* Padding in FFT convolution inducing edge distortions at $t=0$ and $t=T$.\n   * *Mitigation:* Implement linear transient padding and windowed overlap-add during implicit filter evaluation.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Synthesis and Hypotheses Matrix\n\n| Evaluation Dimension | Hypothesized Winner | Mechanism / Justification |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Short Horizon ($T \\le 512$) Accuracy** | Tie / Slight Baseline edge | Full attention has flexible data-dependent weights, but both adequately resolve the phase space. |\n| **Long Horizon ($T \\ge 8192$) Feasibility** | **Hyena-SiT (`setup_5`)** | Baseline SiT crashes due to $\\mathcal{O}(T^2)$ memory wall; Hyena scales as $\\mathcal{O}(T \\log T)$. |\n| **Multi-Timescale Spectral Fidelity** | **Hyena-SiT (`setup_5`)** | Continuous filter MLP explicitly synthesizes multi-frequency basis functions across entire context. |\n| **Attractor & Invariant Consistency** | **Hyena-SiT (`setup_5`)** | Avoids windowing truncation errors, preserving long-range global phase relationships. |\n| **Causal vs. Non-Causal Ablation** | **Non-Causal Hyena** | Non-causality enables bidirectional boundary condition propagation during flow matching trajectory denoising. |\n\nThis comprehensive experimental design provides a rigorous, computationally controlled protocol to answer whether replacing the time attention mechanism with a non-causal Hyena operator enables SiT to generate consistent, multi-timescale trajectories across extreme horizons.", "uncertainties": ["Exact optimal width and depth of the filter MLP for specific extreme frequencies in 2D Kolmogorov flows, which can be fine-tuned via hyperparameter sweeps on validation loss.", "Whether mixed precision FP16/BF16 introduces gradient instability in FFT convolutions at T=16384, mitigated by running FFT operations in full FP32."]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "j4HUaribF9ap4_UPuPC7wQo", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 13}
